  wModel: { title: 'an application', lines: [
    '**APPLICATION · Front of house manager, Hotel Barada, from 1 January · R. Sabbagh, senior receptionist · 11 November**',
    '**WHY I AM WRITING, IN ONE SENTENCE.** **I apply for the post of front of house manager from 1 January, on the strength of four years at this desk in which the complaint rate halved and housekeeping overtime attributable to the desk fell by two thirds.** **Both figures are in the monthly returns and Mr Khoury compiles them; I have not calculated either of them myself for this letter.**',
    '**THREE THINGS I DECIDED, AND WHAT EACH CHANGED.** **Early check-in: free before 11.00, charged after, from May 2023. Housekeeping overtime fell from about 180,000 a month to about 60,000, and the complaint rate did not move — which was the risk and the reason it was worth doing properly rather than quietly.** **Group rooming lists: nothing held over four rooms without writing, from September 2024, which has cost us two small bookings and prevented a repeat of the August loss below.** **The night handover sheet: rewritten in 2024 so that it records decisions rather than events, which is why the hot-water failure in June was found at 02.00 by the porter and not at 07.00 by a guest.**',
    '**WHAT I GOT WRONG, AND WHAT I DID ABOUT IT.** **In August 2024 I let one agent hold twelve rooms on a verbal assurance over four days. The group did not materialise, we released at seven days, and we lost four nights on nine rooms — about 1.1 million.** **What followed is the writing rule above, which has held for fourteen months and which cost us two bookings I would have taken before.** **I include this because an application at this level with no mistake in it tells a reader either that the candidate has not done enough to make one or that something has been left out, and both are worse than the loss.**',
    '**THE GAP BETWEEN THIS DESK AND THAT POST, NAMED BY ME.** **I have never run a payroll and I have never sat in a licensing hearing.** **I have built four rotas against a payroll ceiling and I prepared the annexes for the September inspection, but preparing an annexe is not answering a chair.** **I would want three months’ sight of the payroll alongside whoever runs it now, and I would want to sit behind Mrs Alloush at one hearing before I take one.** **I would rather write that here than be found out at a second interview, and I assume whoever reads forty applications has seen the opposite more than once.**',
    '**THE FIRST NINETY DAYS, AND THEY ARE MODEST.** **One: the decisions in Mrs Alloush’s handover note, each with its revisiting condition, reviewed with her in the first fortnight rather than inherited.** **Two: the unwritten practices in each section written down and given to named people, starting with the two safety items in maintenance.** **Three: a fortnight spent finding out how the restaurant actually books, because I do not know, and a plan I brought with me for a department I have not run would be a description of this desk.**',
    '**AND A NAME AGAINST EVERY CLAIM IN THIS LETTER.** **The overtime and complaint figures: Mr Khoury, monthly returns. The August loss and the release date: Mrs Hourani, who warned me at the time and whose warning is in the file. The handover sheet and the June hot-water call: Mr Tannous. The rotas against the ceiling: Mr Nassar. The September annexes: Mrs Alloush.** **I went down the page and wrote a name beside each claim before sending it, and two sentences came out because I could not name anybody for them.**' ] },
